column8:
Calculate FY 2024 and FY 2025 OEE using Availability, Performance, and Quality derived from the plant dataset; 

compare YoY change to assess whether operational inefficiency contributed materially, noting that a significant increase in OEE rules out inefficiency as a driver.

 Retrieve the 2024 and 2025 annual average coal prices from FRED and compute the external YoY percentage change using annual averages only. 

Compute the internal coal cost YoY change from the cost dataset and calculate the procurement pass-through gap between external and internal movement; 

convert this gap into ₹/MT impact and then into basis points using FY 2024 average realisation as the base. 

Compute similar ₹/MT and bps impacts for freight, power, and raw material (yield) changes, then build a complete margin bridge that reconciles to the total EBITDA decline and rank all drivers by absolute basis point impact; 

if multiple drivers have comparable magnitudes, conclude a multi-driver erosion scenario rather than forcing a single dominant cause.

Golden Values: 
External Coal Price Change = −21.7%; 
Internal Coal Cost Change = −14.2%; 
Procurement Pass-Through Gap = −7.5%;
 Freight Cost Increase = ₹130 per MT; 
Power Cost Increase = ₹98 per MT; 
Raw Material Impact = ₹65 per MT; 
EBITDA Margin Erosion Equivalent = ₹463 per MT (~765 bps); 
OEE Change = +15.1%
